import { ArticleCopy } from "../../../../model/article"

pub fn copy -> ArticleCopy = ArticleCopy {
  title: "Function application",
  summary: "Write a small fn, then call it with arguments separated by spaces. An ordinary call is enough to get started; partial application lets you reuse it with fewer arguments."
}

pub fn title -> String = (copy ()).title

pub fn opening -> String =
  "Let's give addition a name. add takes two integers and returns their sum. Once the function is written, we can use it with different inputs."

pub fn call -> String =
  "Read add 1 2 as ‘give add the values 1 and 2’. The spaces separate arguments; there is no comma or pair of call parentheses. The result is 3. let answer names that result."

pub fn declaration -> String =
  "In the declaration, left: Int and right: Int name the integer arguments. Each arrow continues to the next argument or the result type. The final Int says the function returns an integer; left + right is that returned value."

pub fn display -> String =
  "The calculation stays separate from printing. show answer converts 3 to text, and println describes writing that text with a newline. main returns this Effect; the runner starts it. Parentheses pass the whole show answer calculation as one argument."

pub fn reuse -> String =
  "The same add also accepts add 20 2. Or give it just the first argument: let addOne = add 1. Now addOne is a function waiting for one more integer, so addOne 2 is 3. Supplying only some arguments is called partial application."

pub fn grouping -> String =
  "add 1 2 groups as (add 1) 2. Correspondingly, Int -> Int -> Int means Int -> (Int -> Int): after the first integer, a function of type Int -> Int remains. A -> B -> C follows the same rule; A, B and C stand for arbitrary types here."

pub fn unit -> String =
  "A declaration without a named parameter still takes one Unit value: fn answer -> Int = 42 is called as answer (). Unit means there is no useful input to supply, and () is its only value. The name answer alone still refers to the function."

pub fn generic -> String =
  "You can supply a type explicitly too. fn identity<A> value: A -> A = value returns its argument unchanged. identity<String> \"ready\" chooses String for A. Attach <String> directly to the function name; identity < value uses comparison syntax instead."

pub fn evaluation -> String =
  "The function and its arguments are evaluated once, from left to right. Calling a function that returns an Effect produces a description of work; that call alone does not start the described I/O. Both add arguments are Int, so the types cannot detect that you swapped their roles."

pub fn forms -> String =
  "Use parentheses when a whole compound expression must be one argument: an if expression, a match that selects a data shape, a do expression that sequences operations, or a lambda defined at the call site. A custom binary operator uses spaces, as in left <+> right; (<+>) refers to that operator as a function. Here <+> is notation for an operator you would define separately."

pub fn tuple -> String =
  "add(1, 2) looks familiar in several languages, but (1, 2) is one tuple here. It tries to pass (Int, Int) where add expects its first Int, so the compiler reports SES-T0101. Write add 1 2 to pass two arguments."

pub fn repair -> String =
  "When an application fails, compare what you passed with the declaration: is it one tuple, the wrong type, or a value that is not a function? Adding parentheses does not turn a tuple into two arguments."

pub fn partialExit -> String =
  " — follow the function left after supplying some arguments."
pub fn typesExit -> String = " — choose where to write a type annotation."
pub fn methodExit -> String = " — call from the value using a dot."
pub fn compositionExit -> String =
  " — choose parentheses, $ or a pipeline for nested calls."
pub fn precedenceExit -> String = " — look up how an unparenthesized expression groups."
